  {
   "id": 3,
   "ref": "10–14",
   "en": "What a [stranger|2,7] this is who has come to stay in our home — how noble his [bearing|10,11], how brave his heart, how strong in arms! I really do believe — and it is no idle faith — that his family is divine. Fear betrays a coward's spirit. Ah, what fates he has been driven through! What wars he told of, and endured to the end!'",
   "en_marked": "[What|1] a [stranger|2,7] [this|3] is [who has come to stay in|5] [our|4] [home|6] — [how|8] {noble|Not in the Latin. Added to bring out the admiration in quem sese ore ferens.} [his|9] [bearing|10,11], [how|12] [brave|13] his [heart|14], [how|12] [strong|13] [in arms|16]! [I|17] [really|18] [do believe|17] — [and|19] {it is|Added: the Latin leaves out est, as it often does, so the translator supplies the verb.} [no|19] [idle|20] [faith|21] — that his [family|22] [is|23] [divine|24]. [Fear|27] [betrays|28] a [coward's|25] [spirit|26]. [Ah|29], [what|30] [fates|33] [he|31] [has been driven through|32]! [What|34] [wars|35] [he told of|37], and [endured to the end|36]!'",
