 "notes": [
  {
   "id": "np1-01",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Contrast",
   "quote": "at regina",
   "trans": "but the queen",
   "line": "1",
   "body": "at marks a sharp turn from the end of Book 3, where Aeneas finally falls silent and rests. While he sleeps, the queen does not.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np1-02",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Wound imagery",
   "quote": "saucia … vulnus",
   "trans": "wounded … wound",
   "line": "1–2",
   "body": "Love is pictured as a wound, as if from Cupid's arrow. The image of a wound that will not heal runs through the book and ends in Dido's real wound from Aeneas' sword.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np1-03",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Emphasis",
   "quote": "iamdudum",
   "trans": "for a long time now",
   "line": "1",
   "body": "Her suffering has already lasted all night, perhaps since the banquet in Book 1, so the book opens in the middle of her pain.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np1-04",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Paradox",
   "quote": "vulnus alit venis",
   "trans": "she feeds the wound in her veins",
   "line": "2",
   "body": "A wound should be tended so that it heals; Dido instead feeds it, which shows that she is keeping her love alive even as it harms her.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np1-05",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Fire imagery",
   "quote": "caeco carpitur igni",
   "trans": "she is consumed by a hidden fire",
   "line": "2",
   "body": "The hidden fire eats her away from inside. Fire is the book's main image for Dido's love, and it will end in the real flames of her pyre.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np1-06",
   "cat": "sound",
   "label": "Alliteration",
   "quote": "caeco carpitur",
   "trans": "consumed by a hidden",
   "line": "2",
   "body": "The hard repeated c sounds give a harsh, biting quality to the idea of being eaten away.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np1-07",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Repetition",
   "quote": "multa … multusque",
   "trans": "again and again … again and again",
   "line": "3",
   "body": "multus is repeated at the start of two clauses, so the reader feels Aeneas' qualities returning to Dido's mind over and over.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np1-08",
   "cat": "sound",
   "label": "Alliteration",
   "quote": "viri virtus",
   "trans": "the man's courage",
   "line": "3",
   "body": "The v sounds bind the man to his courage. Dido does not separate the two: she loves Aeneas for his virtus.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np1-09",
   "cat": "order",
   "label": "Enjambment",
   "quote": "haerent infixi pectore vultus / verbaque",
   "trans": "his face and words stay fixed in her heart",
   "line": "4–5",
   "body": "verbaque spills over into the next line, so even the structure shows his words clinging on. infixi, \"driven in\", recalls the arrow and the wound.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np1-10",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Ring structure",
   "quote": "cura … cura",
   "trans": "anxiety … anxiety",
   "line": "1–5",
   "body": "The first sentence opens and closes with cura, enclosing Dido in her love-sickness. At the end, cura denies her body any rest.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np1-11",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Contrast",
   "quote": "Phoebea lustrabat lampade terras",
   "trans": "was lighting the earth with Phoebus' lamp",
   "line": "6",
   "body": "A calm, bright dawn is set against the dark night of Dido's mind. The world wakes refreshed; she has not slept at all.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np1-12",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Characterisation",
   "quote": "unanimam",
   "trans": "of one mind with her",
   "line": "8",
   "body": "Anna is a sister who shares Dido's every thought, which explains why her advice will carry so much weight.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np1-13",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Understatement",
   "quote": "male sana",
   "trans": "hardly sane",
   "line": "8",
   "body": "Literally \"badly sane\": the phrase warns the reader that Dido's judgement is already unsound before she speaks.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np1-15",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Irony",
   "quote": "hospes",
   "trans": "guest, stranger",
   "line": "10",
   "body": "Aeneas is a guest, protected by the laws of hospitality. The word will return with bitter force when Dido feels betrayed by him.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np1-16",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Tricolon",
   "quote": "ore … forti pectore et armis",
   "trans": "his face … his brave heart and arms",
   "line": "11",
   "body": "Three things rising in admiration: his looks, his courage, his skill in war. The breathless exclamations show how dazzled she is.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np1-17",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Dramatic irony",
   "quote": "genus esse deorum",
   "trans": "that he is of the race of the gods",
   "line": "12",
   "body": "Dido is right: Aeneas is the son of Venus. The reader knows what she does not, that this divine family is the cause of her suffering.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np1-18",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Sententia",
   "quote": "degeneres animos timor arguit",
   "trans": "fear exposes a low-born spirit",
   "line": "13",
   "body": "Dido argues like a general: only cowards show fear, so Aeneas' courage proves his noble birth. A short, quotable maxim.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np1-19",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Exclamation",
   "quote": "heu, quibus ille iactatus fatis",
   "trans": "ah, by what fates he has been tossed",
   "line": "13–14",
   "body": "heu and the exclamations show that she pities Aeneas as well as admiring him, and his story of suffering has moved her.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np1-20",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Anna",
   "quote": "Anna",
   "trans": "Anna",
   "line": "9",
   "body": "Dido's sister and closest companion. In some versions of the legend Anna, not Dido, was the one in love with Aeneas; Virgil makes her Dido's confidante and adviser.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np1-21",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Phoebus and Aurora",
   "quote": "Phoebea … Aurora",
   "trans": "of Phoebus … the Dawn",
   "line": "6–7",
   "body": "Phoebus Apollo as god of the sun, and Aurora, goddess of the dawn: an epic way of saying that the next day had begun.",
   "exam": ""
