 "section": 1,
 "questions": [
  "Book 4 opens with Dido already wounded and burning. How does Virgil make her love seem like an illness or an injury from the very first lines?",
  "What does Dido admire in Aeneas in lines 10–14? What does this tell us about her own values?",
  "How far is Dido \"male sana\" (line 8) already? Look at the language of sleeplessness, fear and dreams."
 ],
 "context": [
  {
   "h": "Dido before Book 4",
   "body": "<p>Dido was a princess of Tyre in Phoenicia. Her brother Pygmalion, king of Tyre, murdered her husband Sychaeus for his wealth; Sychaeus' ghost appeared to her in a dream, revealed the crime and showed her where treasure was hidden, and she escaped by sea with a band of followers. In North Africa she bought land from the local king Iarbas and founded Carthage. Venus tells this story to Aeneas in Book 1.</p><p>When Aeneas is shipwrecked on her coast, Dido receives the Trojans generously. At the banquet at the end of Book 1, Venus sends Cupid in the shape of Aeneas' son Ascanius to make Dido fall in love, and through Books 2 and 3 she listens to Aeneas tell the story of the fall of Troy and his wanderings. Book 4 begins the next morning: the damage has already been done.</p>"
  }
 ]
